---
/**
 * Terms.
 *
 * A P0 page from the benchmark page list, and a prerequisite for the pricing page going
 * live: a paid tier cannot open without terms that say what is being bought and what
 * happens when it is not delivered.
 *
 * Everything here states the situation as it is rather than as it will be. Paid
 * subscriptions are not open, so the subscription clauses describe what will apply once
 * they are, and the page says that plainly instead of pretending they are in force. The
 * refund terms follow the same rule as the rest of the site: no ambiguity about what a
 * buyer is entitled to, because ambiguity in a refund policy is what turns a complaint
 * into a chargeback.
 *
 * Not legal advice and not written by a lawyer. It is the clearest statement of the actual
 * arrangement that can be made without one, and it should be reviewed by a lawyer before
 * paid tiers open - which is recorded here rather than left implicit.
 */
import Sidebar from "../components/Sidebar.astro";
import { AUTHOR, SITE } from "../consts";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { numbersFor, published } from "../lib/post";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { fitTitle, fitDescription } from "../lib/meta";

const posts = published(await getCollection("blog"));
const numbers = numbersFor(posts);

const title = "Terms";
const description =
  "What Landed Math is, what a subscription buys, how corrections and refunds work, and the limits of what this analysis can be relied on for.";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: `Terms — ${SITE.name}`,
  description,
  url: `${SITE.url}/terms/`,
};

const UPDATED = posts
  .map((p) => p.data.updatedDate ?? p.data.publishDate)
  .filter(Boolean)
  .sort()
  .pop() ?? "2026-10-08";
---

<BaseLayout title={`${fitTitle(title, "", SITE.name)} — ${SITE.name}`} description={fitDescription(description)} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="container">
    <main id="main">
      <div class="page-head">
        <h1>Terms</h1>
        <p class="updated">Last updated {UPDATED}</p>
        <p>
          Plain terms for a plain product: {posts.length} published analyses, a free tier that
          carries all of them, and — when they open — paid tiers that add cadence and
          convenience. Nothing here is drafted to win an argument later.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout-label">Current status</span>
        <p>
          <strong>The free tier is live. Paid subscriptions are not open and no payment is
          being taken.</strong> The clauses about subscriptions describe what will apply when
          they open. Nothing on this site has charged anyone.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="section-title">1. What this is</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} publishes analysis of Chinese trade policy, platform rules and supply
        chains, converted into per-unit landed cost. Every figure carries a value, a unit, a
        period, a named source and the date it was retrieved. Analysis is published on the open
        web; the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a> lists what is free and what will be paid.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">2. Not advice</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing here is legal, tax, customs, financial or accounting advice, and it is not a
        substitute for a customs broker, a freight forwarder or a lawyer. The cost tables are
        arithmetic on stated inputs — <strong>often inputs you supply</strong>, such as your
        goods value, your funding rate and your storage charge. Change an input and the answer
        changes. Each article's calculation section names the inputs and shows the working, so
        the result can be checked and reproduced rather than taken on trust.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>You are responsible for verifying anything before you rely on it.</strong> Every
        claim traces to a named primary source with a retrieval date, which is exactly so that
        you can check it against the instrument yourself.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">3. Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        When a published figure turns out to be wrong, the article is corrected in public: the
        change is recorded on the article, the date is moved, and the correction is visible
        rather than silent. Each article states which claims are established by a primary
        source and which are the analyst's inference, and each carries a section on what would
        falsify its conclusion.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">4. Subscriptions, when they open</h2>
      <p>
        Paid tiers have not opened yet. When they do, these will apply:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Billing.</strong> Monthly and annual plans renew automatically until cancelled. The price charged is the price shown on the pricing page at the time of purchase.</li>
        <li><strong>Cancelling.</strong> You can cancel at any time and keep access until the end of the period you paid for. No email is required to cancel; a link in every message does it.</li>
        <li><strong>Price changes.</strong> Existing subscribers are told before any price change applies to them, and are not moved onto a higher price mid-term.</li>
        <li><strong>Free tier.</strong> Everything published is free to read in full. A paid tier never removes something that was free when you read it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="section-title">5. Refunds</h2>
      <p>
        See <a href="/refund/">the refund policy</a>. In short: if a paid tier is not what it
        said it was, ask and it is refunded; the policy states the window and the process
        rather than leaving it to discretion.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">6. What you may do with the material</h2>
      <p>
        Read it, quote it, cite it, use the numbers in your own work, and link to it. That is
        the point of publishing figures with their sources. What is not permitted is
        republishing the analysis in full as your own, or reselling it. Quoting a figure with
        its source named is always fine — the citation block on each{" "}
        <a href="/instrument/">instrument page</a> shows the form.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">7. Availability</h2>
      <p>
        The site is a static publication with no servers to go down, but no uptime is promised
        and no service level is offered. Email delivery depends on providers and filters outside
        anyone's control here, which is why there is a page on{" "}
        <a href="/whitelist/">making sure the emails arrive</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">8. Liability</h2>
      <p>
        To the extent permitted by law, {SITE.name} is not liable for commercial decisions
        taken on the basis of this analysis. That is not a disclaimer of responsibility for
        accuracy — the sourcing rules and the correction record are the substance of that — it
        is a statement that a calculation on your inputs cannot be a guarantee about your
        shipment.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title">9. Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Anything on this page, including a refund request or a correction:{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR.email}`}>{AUTHOR.email}</a>. Corrections are welcome and are
        the fastest way to get a figure fixed.
      </p>

      <p class="empty-note">
        Not legal advice, and not drafted by a lawyer: it is the clearest statement of the
        actual arrangement that could be written without one. It should be reviewed by a lawyer
        before paid subscriptions open.
      </p>
    </main>

    <Sidebar numbers={numbers} />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .updated {
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 20px;
  }
  li {
    margin: 7px 0;
  }
</style>
